Hall of No Form (無相殿)
Kong Meng San Phor Kark See Monastery

Preliminary observation
This hall presents the corpus's most significant theoretical challenge to date. The Hall of No Form (無相殿, Wúxiàng Diàn) is named after the doctrinal principle that all forms — including images of the Buddha — have no fixed nature, are empty of self-nature, and arise solely through the coming together of causes and conditions. The hall's name explicitly instructs the devotee that one should not be attached to any form.
This generates an analytical paradox of unusual depth: the hall houses a 13.8-metre, 55-tonne bronze Śākyamuni Buddha as its principal iconographic content, and that very iconographic content is named in such a way as to tell the viewer not to take iconographic content seriously. The hall is iconographically present and doctrinally self-deconstructing in the same act.
This is the second hall at Kong Meng San to encode śūnyatā teaching directly into its name and configuration, after the Hall of Universal Brightness. But where the Hall of Universal Brightness encodes śūnyatā through multiplicity of interpretation (the same three figures simultaneously manifesting three different theological readings), the Hall of No Form encodes śūnyatā through architectural and aesthetic sparseness — by reducing the iconographic programme to a single figure in an unornamented space, it makes the form of the figure stand in tension with the teaching its absence is meant to point toward.
The hall is also institutionally significant in three other respects:
- Located on the FOURTH LEVEL of the Venerable Hong Choon
  Memorial Hall (a multi-storey building separate from the
  Pagoda of Ten Thousand Buddhas)

- Spatially extensive: 3,290 square metres, capacity 2,000 people
  -- approximately half a football field

- Pillarless interior: open, bright, unobstructed
  -- architectural emptiness as material correlate of doctrinal emptiness

- Functionally specialized: Dharma talks, symposiums,
  meditation sessions, prayer services, Threefold Refuge ceremonies,
  Five Precepts ceremonies
  -- a teaching/practice hall rather than primarily a devotional shrine

Step 1 — ICON description
ICON₁ — Śākyamuni Buddha (釋迦牟尼佛) (Central, principal and only deity)
POSITION    : central, axial, elevated
SCALE       : dominant
              (13.8m from lotus base, 55 tonnes;
               also: SINGULAR within the entire vast hall)
ORIENTATION : front-facing
FUNCTION    : soteriological, devotional, pedagogical, cosmological
ICONOGRAPHIC REGISTER : Chinese Mahāyāna (Taiwanese-crafted bronze)
The principal Buddha is seated in meditation posture (dhyāna mudrā), in classical Chinese Mahāyāna sculptural form, made in Taiwan. The scale is monumental — the figure rises 13.8 metres from the lotus base. But the most striking feature of the configuration is what surrounds the Buddha: nothing iconographic. The dome above is plain. The walls are bare. The space is pillarless. There are no flanking attendants, no guardian figures, no lateral altars, no thresholds marked by figurative iconography, no decorative ceiling programmes, no narrative friezes. Just one Buddha, a vast empty hall, and the worshipping community that gathers within it.
The SCALE assignment requires careful note. The Buddha is dominant in the absolute sense — there is no other ICON in the hall against which to be compared. Within RFIG's vocabulary this is singular — but singular has previously been used for figures in small individual shrines without spatial competitors. Here the Buddha is singular within a hall designed to accommodate two thousand people, which produces a different effect: not a small figure alone in a small space, but a colossal figure alone in a vast space, generating an aesthetic of monumental solitude.

