Step 4 — The negation operator and grammar refinement
This hall makes explicit a grammatical extension that has been used implicitly in earlier analyses: the negation operator (¬) on RELATION statements. The Administrative Quarters analysis introduced ¬mirrors to record the absence of a bilateral counterpart (subsequently retracted when the Ritual Hall was identified as its pair). Here negation operates differently — not to record an absent expected relation, but to record an actively negated relation.
¬manifests
  -- The relation is deliberately and doctrinally negated.
     The hall is not silent about whether the Buddha-form
     manifests fixed Buddha-nature; it actively claims that
     it does not. The negation is part of the iconographic
     programme.
This is the corpus's first instance where doctrinal teaching takes the form of a negated iconographic claim. The hall does not simply lack mandorlas, attendants, or lateral programmes; it teaches that they would be misleading. To populate the hall with the kinds of iconographic abundance documented in (for example) the Hall of Amrita Precepts would, on the doctrinal logic the hall's name asserts, contradict its theological purpose.
The grammar therefore needs to distinguish three modes of relational absence:
1. Simple absence: no relation of this type holds
   (no analytical claim is made)

2. Deliberate non-presence: a relation that would be expected
   given context is not present
   (e.g., an unpaired bilateral structure)

3. Actively negated: the relation is doctrinally rejected
   (¬manifests, ¬centers, etc. — the absence is the teaching)
The Hall of No Form deploys mode 3 across its entire iconographic programme. Every absence — of attendants, of mandorlas, of lateral altars, of decorative cosmography, of pillared structure — is theologically motivated. The hall teaches through the relations it does not deploy.
